WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.265
Lesson 1.7.

00:00:02.265 --> 00:00:05.440
Reading signatures and simulating transactions.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.172
By the end, you'll know exactly what a signature

00:00:08.172 --> 00:00:11.015
or transaction will do, before you ever approve it.

00:00:12.315 --> 00:00:13.605
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.605 --> 00:00:15.912
Simulation, which previews the balance

00:00:15.912 --> 00:00:18.165
changes before you sign anything.

00:00:18.165 --> 00:00:20.678
Typed-data signatures, and the four fields

00:00:20.678 --> 00:00:22.965
worth reading on every single one.

00:00:22.965 --> 00:00:24.449
Blind signing, and why it

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:26.290
should stay off by default.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:29.767
And account delegation, the newest and most powerful thing

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:32.007
a signature can now do to your wallet.

00:00:33.307 --> 00:00:35.112
One rule to hold onto through

00:00:35.112 --> 00:00:37.517
all four topics, before the detail.

00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:39.395
If you can't say, in one plain

00:00:39.395 --> 00:00:42.182
sentence, what a prompt actually does, that's

00:00:42.182 --> 00:00:44.057
not a detail to skip past.

00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:46.187
It's the answer already: don't sign it.

00:00:47.487 --> 00:00:48.442
Simulation.

00:00:49.742 --> 00:00:52.902
Here's what a good wallet, or a transaction simulator

00:00:52.902 --> 00:00:56.187
built into one, actually does before you ever sign.

00:00:56.187 --> 00:00:58.392
You ask for an action, say a swap.

00:00:58.392 --> 00:01:01.377
Before showing you the signing prompt, it runs that

00:01:01.377 --> 00:01:05.752
exact transaction against current chain state, without broadcasting it.

00:01:05.752 --> 00:01:07.686
And it shows you the result in

00:01:07.686 --> 00:01:11.292
plain terms: balances leaving, and balances arriving.

00:01:11.292 --> 00:01:13.827
You're not guessing what a contract call does.

00:01:13.827 --> 00:01:15.862
You're looking at the outcome, in advance.

00:01:17.162 --> 00:01:19.113
So here's the check that actually

00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:21.766
matters, every time that preview appears.

00:01:21.766 --> 00:01:25.751
A normal, expected preview shows exactly what you intended,

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:29.306
one asset out, one asset in, nothing else moving.

00:01:29.306 --> 00:01:31.539
A warning sign is a preview showing an

00:01:31.539 --> 00:01:34.516
asset leaving that you never mentioned, an approval

00:01:34.516 --> 00:01:37.092
appearing you didn't ask for, or your entire

00:01:37.092 --> 00:01:39.096
balance of something being touched.

00:01:40.397 --> 00:01:42.590
Not every wallet or every action has

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.139
a simulator attached, so here's the manual

00:01:45.139 --> 00:01:47.692
fallback when you don't get a preview.

00:01:47.692 --> 00:01:49.472
Look up the contract on a block

00:01:49.472 --> 00:01:51.997
explorer first, and check it's verified.

00:01:51.997 --> 00:01:54.549
Read the actual function name the transaction

00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:57.012
calls, not just the button you clicked.

00:01:57.012 --> 00:01:59.801
And start with a small test amount before ever

00:01:59.801 --> 00:02:03.242
sending your full position through an unfamiliar contract.

00:02:04.542 --> 00:02:05.632
Quick check.

00:02:05.632 --> 00:02:08.102
A simulator previews your swap and shows

00:02:08.102 --> 00:02:10.992
an unrelated token leaving your wallet too.

00:02:10.992 --> 00:02:11.842
What do you do?

00:02:16.262 --> 00:02:17.857
The answer: stop.

00:02:17.857 --> 00:02:20.061
That's not the transaction you asked for,

00:02:20.061 --> 00:02:22.212
whatever the interface claims it's doing.

00:02:23.512 --> 00:02:24.937
Typed-data signatures.

00:02:26.237 --> 00:02:28.808
First, what a typed-data signature actually

00:02:28.808 --> 00:02:31.153
is, since “Permit” sounds harmless.

00:02:31.153 --> 00:02:34.986
It's a standard called E.I.P. seven-twelve: a structured

00:02:34.986 --> 00:02:37.944
message, like an order or a token approval,

00:02:37.944 --> 00:02:40.768
that your wallet can show you as readable

00:02:40.768 --> 00:02:42.951
fields instead of raw bytes.

00:02:42.951 --> 00:02:47.031
A “Permit” or “Permit2” signature specifically can hand over

00:02:47.031 --> 00:02:51.211
token access without any transaction, and without any gas.

00:02:51.211 --> 00:02:53.894
That's exactly why it's worth reading carefully;

00:02:53.894 --> 00:02:55.701
nothing about the cost warns you.

00:02:57.001 --> 00:02:59.102
Here are the four fields that matter

00:02:59.102 --> 00:03:01.486
on every single typed-data prompt, in the

00:03:01.486 --> 00:03:03.376
order to actually read them.

00:03:03.376 --> 00:03:05.569
Spender: which address is being given

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:07.716
access, and do you recognise it.

00:03:07.716 --> 00:03:09.946
Token: which asset this covers.

00:03:09.946 --> 00:03:13.848
Amount: a specific number, or “unlimited.” And deadline: how

00:03:13.848 --> 00:03:17.346
long this grant stays valid, sometimes years away.

00:03:17.346 --> 00:03:20.451
Read all four, every time, before you sign anything.

00:03:21.751 --> 00:03:23.931
Put those four fields into a decision

00:03:23.931 --> 00:03:26.296
you can actually make in the moment.

00:03:26.296 --> 00:03:28.454
Read the spender first, and if it's

00:03:28.454 --> 00:03:31.211
unfamiliar, that alone is reason to stop.

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:33.718
Check whether the amount matches what this

00:03:33.718 --> 00:03:36.351
one action needs, or reads as unlimited.

00:03:36.351 --> 00:03:38.444
Check the deadline: real actions

00:03:38.444 --> 00:03:40.646
rarely need years of validity.

00:03:40.646 --> 00:03:43.148
And if any of the three feels wrong, reject

00:03:43.148 --> 00:03:46.446
and re-check, rather than assume the interface knows best.

00:03:47.746 --> 00:03:51.247
Worth knowing why “Permit” signatures exist at all, since

00:03:51.247 --> 00:03:54.021
it's a real convenience, not just a risk.

00:03:54.021 --> 00:03:56.502
A classic approval is its own on-chain

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:59.301
transaction: it costs gas, and you'd notice

00:03:59.301 --> 00:04:01.001
it as a separate step.

00:04:01.001 --> 00:04:03.534
A Permit or Permit2 signature does the

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:06.653
same job, off-chain and free, bundled invisibly

00:04:06.653 --> 00:04:09.551
into the action you were already taking.

00:04:09.551 --> 00:04:11.681
Convenient when you understand it.

00:04:11.681 --> 00:04:13.826
Exactly why it also needs reading.

00:04:15.126 --> 00:04:16.216
Quick check.

00:04:16.216 --> 00:04:18.967
What can a “Permit” signature grant, without

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:20.801
any transaction or gas at all?

00:04:25.221 --> 00:04:28.028
The answer: token access, for the spender,

00:04:28.028 --> 00:04:31.031
amount and duration written into the message,

00:04:31.031 --> 00:04:33.511
exactly as shown in those four fields.

00:04:34.811 --> 00:04:36.741
Blind signing, and delegation.

00:04:38.041 --> 00:04:39.874
Here's the distinction that matters

00:04:39.874 --> 00:04:41.906
on a hardware wallet specifically.

00:04:41.906 --> 00:04:45.195
Normal signing decodes the transaction and shows you

00:04:45.195 --> 00:04:48.847
readable fields, spender, amount, deadline, the works.

00:04:48.847 --> 00:04:51.435
Blind signing is what happens when the device

00:04:51.435 --> 00:04:53.573
can't decode it: you see only a hash,

00:04:53.573 --> 00:04:56.160
a string of characters that tells you nothing

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.242
about what you're actually approving.

00:04:59.542 --> 00:05:01.353
So here's the actual rule for

00:05:01.353 --> 00:05:03.222
blind signing, as a habit.

00:05:03.222 --> 00:05:04.758
Keep it off by default,

00:05:04.758 --> 00:05:06.786
every day, on every device.

00:05:06.786 --> 00:05:08.348
Turn it on only for one

00:05:08.348 --> 00:05:11.836
specific, already-verified action you understand.

00:05:11.836 --> 00:05:13.546
Complete that one action.

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:15.589
Then turn it straight back off, before

00:05:15.589 --> 00:05:16.951
you forget you left it on.

00:05:18.251 --> 00:05:21.662
Now the newest risk: account delegation, under

00:05:21.662 --> 00:05:25.870
a standard called E.I.P. seventy-seven-oh-two, live since

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.436
Ethereum's twenty twenty-five Pectra upgrade.

00:05:29.436 --> 00:05:31.921
Normally, a wallet is just a key.

00:05:31.921 --> 00:05:35.099
This lets that same wallet sign an authorisation

00:05:35.099 --> 00:05:39.291
that delegates its behaviour to smart-contract code instead.

00:05:39.291 --> 00:05:42.611
A legitimate app might use this for useful features.

00:05:42.611 --> 00:05:45.688
A malicious site can use the exact same signature

00:05:45.688 --> 00:05:48.641
to hand your entire account over to an attacker.

00:05:49.941 --> 00:05:52.451
And here's what actually happens next, once

00:05:52.451 --> 00:05:55.376
a malicious delegation signature goes through.

00:05:55.376 --> 00:05:57.364
The attacker's contract is now the

00:05:57.364 --> 00:06:00.106
code controlling your account, immediately.

00:06:00.106 --> 00:06:02.895
It can move every asset the account holds, without

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:05.766
asking for a single further signature from you.

00:06:05.766 --> 00:06:07.659
This often happens within minutes

00:06:07.659 --> 00:06:09.796
of the signing, sometimes faster.

00:06:09.796 --> 00:06:12.170
And once it's done, it's done: there's no

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:14.091
undo, and no support line to call.

00:06:15.391 --> 00:06:18.700
So the rule for delegation signatures is narrower than

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.707
for anything else in this lesson, on purpose.

00:06:21.707 --> 00:06:23.857
Only sign one from a wallet feature

00:06:23.857 --> 00:06:25.768
or app you already trust, for a

00:06:25.768 --> 00:06:28.337
purpose you can state in one sentence.

00:06:28.337 --> 00:06:30.228
An unfamiliar site asking for a

00:06:30.228 --> 00:06:32.892
delegation isn't offering you a feature.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:34.620
It's asking for control of the

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.682
whole account, in a single signature.

00:06:37.982 --> 00:06:40.916
Delegation itself isn't automatically the enemy

00:06:40.916 --> 00:06:43.417
here, so here's the honest contrast.

00:06:43.417 --> 00:06:46.843
A legitimate use is a wallet feature you deliberately

00:06:46.843 --> 00:06:50.205
chose, upgrading your own account to add multisig or

00:06:50.205 --> 00:06:53.487
spending limits, from an app you already trust.

00:06:53.487 --> 00:06:56.574
A malicious one is an unfamiliar site asking for

00:06:56.574 --> 00:07:00.101
the exact same signature, with no feature explained, no

00:07:00.101 --> 00:07:02.810
purpose you could state back in a sentence.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:05.200
Quick check.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.545
Your hardware wallet shows only a hash

00:07:07.545 --> 00:07:10.129
for a transaction, no readable fields.

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:11.834
What should your default response be?

00:07:16.254 --> 00:07:18.019
The answer: don't sign it.

00:07:18.019 --> 00:07:20.671
Keep blind signing off, and only enable it

00:07:20.671 --> 00:07:23.509
briefly for one action you already understand.

00:07:24.809 --> 00:07:25.814
Worked example.

00:07:27.114 --> 00:07:32.122
A site asks you to “verify your wallet.” Your

00:07:32.122 --> 00:07:39.090
wallet shows a typed-data message: Permit2, token USDC, spender

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:46.684
0x9f3a…c3, amount 115792089… (the unlimited number), deadline 2030.

00:07:46.684 --> 00:07:50.359
Read those four fields and the trick collapses instantly.

00:07:50.359 --> 00:07:52.184
This isn't verification.

00:07:52.184 --> 00:07:55.672
It's unlimited USDC access, for a spender you've

00:07:55.672 --> 00:07:58.234
never seen, for the next five years.

00:07:59.534 --> 00:08:01.555
One real, dated fact behind why

00:08:01.555 --> 00:08:03.749
this exact scam works so well.

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:09.551
Security researchers have documented Permit and Permit2 phishing signatures

00:08:09.551 --> 00:08:14.360
draining six- and seven-figure sums from individual wallets in

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.714
a single approval, across 2023 and 2024.

00:08:17.714 --> 00:08:19.703
Not a rare edge case: one of the

00:08:19.703 --> 00:08:23.077
highest-value theft techniques currently in use, and it

00:08:23.077 --> 00:08:25.789
starts with exactly the prompt you just read.

00:08:27.089 --> 00:08:29.175
So here's exactly what you do with that

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:31.249
prompt in front of you, right now.

00:08:31.249 --> 00:08:34.894
Reject the signature, don't sign it under any circumstance.

00:08:34.894 --> 00:08:37.140
Close the tab completely; don't navigate

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:38.974
elsewhere on that same site.

00:08:38.974 --> 00:08:42.081
And go check your existing approvals afterward, since a

00:08:42.081 --> 00:08:44.633
site willing to try this once may have others

00:08:44.633 --> 00:08:46.519
live from before you knew to look.

00:08:47.819 --> 00:08:51.149
And here's what “check your existing approvals” actually

00:08:51.149 --> 00:08:53.954
means as a walk-through, since it's worth doing

00:08:53.954 --> 00:08:56.474
on a schedule, not just after a scare.

00:08:56.474 --> 00:08:59.820
Open an approval-checker tool for your wallet address.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.803
Review the full list of spenders it

00:09:01.803 --> 00:09:04.030
shows you, not just the newest one.

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:06.514
Revoke anything unfamiliar, or anything

00:09:06.514 --> 00:09:08.615
unlimited you no longer need.

00:09:08.615 --> 00:09:10.852
And put a reminder in your calendar to

00:09:10.852 --> 00:09:13.835
repeat this, since old approvals accumulate quietly.

00:09:15.135 --> 00:09:16.595
Here's your checklist.

00:09:16.595 --> 00:09:18.130
Do it now, for real.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.914
Your wallet shows simulated balance changes

00:09:20.914 --> 00:09:22.985
before you sign, every time.

00:09:22.985 --> 00:09:25.353
You read spender, token, amount and

00:09:25.353 --> 00:09:28.020
deadline on every typed-data prompt.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:29.494
Blind signing stays off on

00:09:29.494 --> 00:09:31.540
your hardware wallet by default.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:34.528
And you never sign an account-delegation request from

00:09:34.528 --> 00:09:36.630
a site or app you don't already trust.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:39.175
Let's recap.

00:09:39.175 --> 00:09:42.130
Four ideas, one shared habit underneath them.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:45.780
Simulation previews the outcome before you commit to it.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:48.111
Typed-data signatures reduce to four

00:09:48.111 --> 00:09:50.490
fields worth reading, every time.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:53.166
Blind signing hides those fields, so it stays

00:09:53.166 --> 00:09:56.200
off unless you've verified the action yourself.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.909
And account delegation is the newest, most powerful signature

00:09:59.909 --> 00:10:02.780
of all, worth the narrowest trust rule you have.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.026
Read every prompt, and simulate before you sign,

00:10:07.026 --> 00:10:09.370
and the vast majority of what could go

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.135
wrong on-chain simply stops reaching you.

00:10:12.135 --> 00:10:16.305
Next up, Lesson 1.8: Privacy and physical security.
